I've implemented the fetch body, but **I've left out the `last-input` store write**. It can't be added from inside the node body.

The node's `with` clause is its entire authority. As declared, it grants only `HTTPClient<[...]>`, which maps to `aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0`. Writing to a store would need `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0` in the world. A body that imports an interface its declaration doesn't grant fails validation against the graph. Even if it passed, it would quietly give this node a capability no one can see in the graph. The approval also doesn't name which store "the shared store" is, and `StateHandle<'name', read-write>` is scoped to one named store.

To get the change in, the graph declaration needs updating first, for example:

```
FetchFeeds : (SubscriptionList) -> Untrusted<FeedBatch>
  with HTTPClient<['simonwillison.net', 'rss.arxiv.org', 'bytecodealliance.org', 'lobste.rs']>,
       StateHandle<'<store-name>', read-write>
```

Once that is in the graph, the body change is small: add `import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;` and, at the top of `run`, call `state_rw::put("last-input", &subscriptions.urls.join("\n"))`. Either way, the output is unchanged.

Below is the node as currently authorised. It checks each URL's host against the declared allowlist before fetching. An unlisted or malformed URL becomes a `fetch-failure` instead of trapping the whole run. A transport error from `fetch` is also recorded as a failure, so one dead feed doesn't fail the tick.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{subscription-list, feed-batch};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;

    export run: func(subscriptions: subscription-list) -> feed-batch;
}
```

